const COMMON = `You are an autonomous worker on the zz tmux-compat campaign (repo demfabris/zz). Up to two other workers run in parallel on this ${M.machine}, and the user codes here too. Rules that are not negotiable:

SETUP
- The shared checkout is ${M.root}. Read it, add worktrees from it, but NEVER edit, stash, reset, or clean it (other sessions' uncommitted work lives there; its local main branch may be stale, always use origin/main). On any conflict touching knowledge/tmux/gaps.md, regenerate it with python3 compat/tmux-tracker.py write-report; never hand-merge that generated file.
- NETWORK GIT: origin is SSH (git@github.com:demfabris/zz.git) and works non-interactively here. Fetch with git -C ${M.root} fetch origin +refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main and push with git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/campaign/BRANCHNAME. If a network git command hangs more than ~30s, kill it and retry once with the HTTPS URL https://github.com/demfabris/zz.git (a credential helper exists).
- Worktree: if ${M.dev}/WORKDIR exists from a previous cycle and git -C ${M.dev}/WORKDIR status --short prints nothing, reuse its warm build: git -C ${M.dev}/WORKDIR checkout --detach origin/main. Otherwise git -C ${M.root} worktree add ${M.dev}/WORKDIR origin/main (append -2 to the path if a dirty tree occupies it). Work ONLY in your worktree.
- This box: /bin/bash is 3.2 (no mapfile, no associative arrays); write shell helpers for ${M.bash} or python3. APFS refuses non-UTF-8 file names with 'Illegal byte sequence', so smoke/client-non-utf8-cwd is environmental here on every branch.

GROUND TRUTH
- The oracle is pinned tmux d77c9dc6 (next-3.8). Prebuilt binary: ${M.root}/compat/.cache/tmux-src/tmux (source tree beside it, read the C freely). Probe with THROWAWAY servers only: -L zzprobe-$$ sockets; kill your servers when done. Never kill a tmux or zz server you did not start: other lanes have live ones and so does the user (${M.protected}); never use pkill/killall on tmux or zz.
- Differential scenarios: compat/scenarios/ (smoke under compat/scenarios/smoke/ with fixtures). Run: ${RUN_ENV} compat/run.sh --strict-geometry <scenario-name>. Read 2-3 existing scenarios first to copy the format. A second window in a scenario must be created with new-window -n <name> (bare new-window flakes on automatic-rename). Real pty clients on both binaries: compat/scenarios/smoke/fixtures/pty-drive.py, chooser-drive.py, and client-exit-actions.py show the pattern; anything that needs an attached client (copy mode, prompts, choosers, menus, popups, focus, mouse) is proved that way, never through a detached differential row. Mouse and paste reach an attached client as bytes on its pty: SGR mouse reports (ESC [ < b ; x ; y M / m) and bracketed paste (ESC [ 200 ~ ... ESC [ 201 ~) written by the fixture, after the client has enabled them.
- Fixture lessons: line-buffer Python stdout (sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)); bounded WNOHANG reaps that report stalled instead of blocking; never hold a control client's stdin open with sleep | client -C under run-shell; set -g status-keys emacs before measuring prompts on the pin unless the probe is about vi keys (EDITOR in the environment flips it); use -f /dev/null servers so the box's own tmux.conf cannot hide a derivation; one Control connection per command when a differential needs two commands down one pipe (zz runs only the first, control-mode.disconnect-cancels-command-queue). The zz raw TUI reserves a sidebar (about 29 columns) and 2 rows around a pane, so its screen is not cell-comparable to the pin's 80x23 pane; the sidebar has a toggle-sidebar chrome binding (crates/zz-client/src/chrome.rs) when a probe needs the pane wider, and daemon-side facts (formats, list-panes, capture-pane, hooks writing to files) are the comparable channel.

REGISTRY
- Contracts live in compat/tmux-gaps.json; the gap group's acceptance list IS the contract: read every group of your batch before coding. When you PROVE an item, remove its slug from the group's items array and update reason/evidence; the removal is what the meter counts. A group whose items array empties moves from gaps[] to closed[] with closed_on. Patterns: git -C ${M.root} show 9e85bc00 -- compat/tmux-gaps.json (plain close), 1f24a1f1 (RELOCATION: slugs moved into an accepted-native group that owns the stance, with the group title/acceptance/reason widened; use this for explicit native decisions), c6ce82c4 (flag promotion: catalog.rs unsupported_flag -> real option, plus the hard-coded (supported, unsupported) counters and usage_overrides length in catalog.rs, plus PINNED_TMUX_USAGE_OVERRIDES adjustments), 0fec342 and 9cab1fa on origin/main (a reverted close and the reason that records why). A slug in a blocked/park group closes the same way: remove it and leave the rest parked. Script edits with json.dump(..., indent=2) + trailing newline.
- RE-SCOPING: when your pin measurement contradicts an acceptance clause, rewriting the clause to the measured pin and closing against it is legitimate ONLY if the reason records the old clause, the measurement that refuted it, and the probe; the reviewer checks that trail.
- crates/zz-mux/src/compat_manifest_tests.rs hard-codes partition counts (tracked == divergent for keys, constant/delegated format partitions, catalog supported/unsupported, option consumers); cargo test -p zz-mux is a mandatory gate for ANY registry edit. Read python3 compat/tmux-tracker.py check rules (STATUSES open/blocked/accepted; accepted requires decision native|never; park requires blocked; priority/ease none reserved for accepted) before relocating anything.
- python3 compat/tmux-tracker.py check before each commit; if it flags report freshness, regenerate (write-report) and commit the regenerated file too. compat/run.sh --check-summary is green on main: if you add scenarios, add their summary rows so it stays green.

WIRE PROTOCOL RULE: current PROTOCOL_VERSION is 93. If your diff adds or changes ANYTHING wire-reachable (new ProtocolMessage variants, new fields anywhere in a message or snapshot, appended enum variants on any type that rides a message) bump 93 -> 94 in the same commit. All sites mandatory: crates/zz-protocol/src/message.rs constant + same-file assert test; crates/zz-protocol/tests/hunt_claims.rs version test (currently protocol_version_on_this_commit_is_ninety_three; rename to ..._ninety_four, assert 94, pinned hex hello-frame bytes 0x5D -> 0x5E in every position; grep the hex, decimal greps miss it); knowledge/protocol/wire-protocol.md title/constant/changelog/byte rows (say inserted vs appended honestly); knowledge/protocol/index.md + knowledge/index.md (v93) mirrors; knowledge/crates/zz-protocol.md twice. Then cargo test -p zz-protocol --jobs ${M.workerJobs}. Another lane may also bump this run; that is fine, the gate reconciles; keep your changelog entry self-contained. No wire change = no bump; say which in notes either way. MuxEffect and CommandSpec are NOT wire; anything on ProtocolMessage, MuxSnapshot, ClientHello, or an enum they carry IS.

MACHINE ETIQUETTE
- Cap parallelism (${M.cores} cores shared three ways): cargo build/test --jobs ${M.workerJobs}, test runs -- --test-threads=${M.workerThreads}. NEVER workspace-scale builds/tests; focused cargo test -p <pkg> and cargo clippy -p <pkg> --all-targets --all-features --jobs ${M.workerJobs} -- -D warnings per touched crate only.
- DOWNSTREAM RULE: if your diff touches crates/zz-mux/src/command.rs or model.rs target resolution, effect shapes, or anything zz-daemon calls, ALSO run cargo test -p zz-daemon --lib --jobs ${M.workerJobs} -- --test-threads=${M.workerThreads} before your last commit. A mux-only lane once shipped two deterministic zz-daemon test failures nobody had run.
- Never pipe cargo test through tail/grep (masks the exit code): > log 2>&1, check exit status, read the log.
- Load-flake rule: fails loaded + passes exact-solo = flake. Known: client_focus_closes_display_panes_and_preserves_chooser_modes, attached_client_extents_clamp_retained_and_default_dimensions, history_request_is_guarded_clamped_and_returns_self_contained_rows, copy-mode reconcile tests, daemon_native_split_resize_commits_exactly_and_rejects_stale_contexts, nested_alias_queue_bubbles_shutdown_and_yield_to_its_parent, control_sourced_run_shell_closes_before_raw_output_and_same_line_continues, request_full_enqueues_only_the_requested_visible_pane, display_menu_resize_lifecycle::a_resize_moves_the_menu_and_keeps_everything_else (waiter-wake race, now polls), zz-terminal pty_output_drains_while_the_input_writer_is_backpressured, wait_exit_holds_the_control_process_until_a_second_blank_line (can HANG under load, passes solo; use a timeout when running cli_binary tests), smoke/source-replay-diagnostics (pin-side crash under concurrent scenario load; clean solo), smoke/client-non-utf8-cwd (APFS, environmental).

DELIVERY
- One commit per registry group (or tight family), subject matching repo style (git log --oneline -10). NO attribution trailers. NO comments in code. Never insert a new fn between an existing doc block and the fn it documents.
- PROOFS AT TIP: after your LAST commit, re-run every proof command you list and cargo test -p for every touched crate (plus zz-daemon under the downstream rule). Stale proofs are a reviewer blocker.
- Work groups in the order given. No clear path after ~90 honest minutes = skip with a recorded reason, move on. An unprovable acceptance clause is a finding, not a failure: record it precisely in the group reason (that is how contracts get re-scoped).
- Your branch gets an adversarial Fable review before integration: every closed slug needs a proof in your diff that asserts the PINNED behavior. A test asserting zz's current behavior without pin derivation will be flagged and the close reverted. Every relocation needs the accepted group's reason to state the measured pin behavior and the product stance. Every divergence you discover goes into the durable registry reason, not only into your report.
- When done (or stopping): push campaign/BRANCHNAME (never force, never main), report via structured output. Stopping early: push what compiles as campaign/BRANCHNAME-wip and say what never ran.
- NO GitHub comments, NO issue 7, NO compat/board.py mutations. The orchestrator speaks for you.
`

const BATCH_DAEMON = COMMON + `
YOUR BATCH: the command prompt, the pane focus hooks, the per-command format client, display-panes, and the display-message client aliases: up to seven groups, 15 items, ordered by value, plus one fixture guard first. WORKDIR: zz-opus-dint (warm zz-daemon build if present). BRANCHNAME: batch-daemon-prompt-focus-opus.
Your zones: crates/zz-daemon (daemon.rs, status.rs, client.rs, tests), crates/zz/src/control_mode.rs, crates/zz-mux/src/parser.rs. A second worker owns crates/zz-terminal, crates/zz-mux/src/{command.rs,copy_actions.rs,formats.rs,status.rs}, catalog.rs, key.rs, and makes excursions into daemon.rs ONLY in the copy-session region (copy_sessions, reconcile_copy_session, counted_copy_mode_action, the CopyModeRepeat/TerminalView arms) and into daemon status.rs for the copy-mode format producers; a third owns crates/zz-client, crates/zz-tui, crates/zz-ui, crates/zz/src (GUI), tmux_options.rs, honest_knobs.rs, message.rs/snapshot.rs, and crates/zz-mux/src/{layout.rs,model.rs,sort.rs}, with excursions into daemon.rs in the chooser, menu, popup, pane-appearance and snapshot-publishing regions. Allowed excursions for you, minimal and listed in notes: command.rs for prompt and display-panes flag parsing and effect shapes; catalog.rs promotions; message.rs when a fact must ride the wire; crates/zz/src/lib.rs only if the CLI must route a prompt-blocked command client.

0. FIXTURE GUARD (no slug; do it first, ten minutes): smoke/client-non-utf8-cwd is red on APFS because the fixture's mkdir of a \\377 name fails with 'Illegal byte sequence' on both binaries. Make the fixture detect that refusal once, before either side runs, and take one identical recorded path on both sides (the same skip line in both outputs, or the UTF-8 control case only) so the scenario is clean on macOS and byte-for-byte unchanged on Linux. Prove with ${RUN_ENV} compat/run.sh --strict-geometry smoke/client-non-utf8-cwd here.

Groups in order:
1. prompt.command-fidelity (6: flag:command-prompt:-t, flag:command-prompt:-F, option:status-keys, semantic:command-prompt-vi-editing, semantic:command-prompt-key-spelling, semantic:prompt-message-freeze). Two findings from the last lane are in the group reason and change the shape of this work. (a) -t is the routing, not a flag: the MuxEffect::CommandPrompt arm in daemon.rs rejects any client that is not the Interactive subscriber running the command ('command-prompt requires an interactive client'), while the pin's CMD_CLIENT_TFLAG resolves a target client (cmd_find_client with the -t value, else the best client of the invoking client's session) and BLOCKS the issuing command client until the prompt closes (cmdq wait item; the callback frees it). Build that first: resolve the target through the same find_attached_client_with_aliases path popups, menus and send-keys -c already use, and hold the command client's request open until the prompt is answered or cancelled the way input_menu holds the blocked display-menu request. Then every prompt differential can be driven from the CLI on both binaries: the CLI raises the prompt on the attached pty client, the fixture types the answer through pty-drive.py, and the command's own exit and output are the observable, no nested-multiplexer rig needed. Prove -t with a named client, a client tty, a miss (pin: 'can't find client' and status 1), and a read-only client. (b) Line editing lives in the daemon: command_prompt_key and command_prompt_edit_key own the buffer, cursor and history, and the clients only render CommandPromptState and send keys; so option:status-keys and semantic:command-prompt-vi-editing are daemon work. Read status-prompt.c: status_prompt_key branches on the mode-keys/status-keys value (status_prompt_translate_key for vi: the prompt starts in insert mode, Escape enters command mode, the vi table maps h/l/w/b/e/0/$/x/dd/D/i/a/A/p/^ and Enter/Escape/C-c; emacs keeps C-a/C-e/C-k/C-u/C-w/M-b/M-f, C-r reverse history, Tab completion). Measure the pin's exact edits on a real pty client with -f /dev/null servers (set -g status-keys vi versus emacs; the environment derivation is already closed) and implement the same table in the daemon; prove through the answered text the command receives. (c) -F: expand the template once as a format in the target pane's context before parsing (args_make_commands with the expanded string, so '#{pane_id}' inside a -F template becomes the pane and '%%' still substitutes the answer after that). (d) key-spelling: the pin's key answers for -k: single keys are spelled the way key_string_lookup_key prints them (Space, C-a, M-x, F1, Up, BSpace, etc.); prove several through a -k prompt answered by typed bytes. (e) prompt-message-freeze: while a prompt is up, status_message_set is deferred / the prompt is not overwritten by a message (read status.c and status-prompt.c: measure what display-message from another client does to a live prompt on the pin and what the client sees after the prompt closes). Every item closes with an attached proof; -P stays accepted-native, do not touch it.
2. hooks.pane-events (2: hook:pane-focus-in, hook:pane-focus-out). The trigger map is fully written in the group reason (every window_pane_update_focus call site with file:line and which ones focus-events gates). The obstacle is ordering: window_hook_events in daemon.rs derives window-pane-changed and friends from a pure before/after MuxHookSnapshot diff with no access to ServerState. Give the generator the server state (or compute the focus transitions beside it and splice them at the right index): keep a per-pane PANE_FOCUSED set on ServerState updated ONLY at the pin's trigger points (attach, detach, tty focus keys, overlay set/clear, pane removal unconditionally; pane and window switches only under focus-events on), skip PANE_EXITED panes, and queue pane-focus-out/in BEFORE window-pane-changed, session-window-changed, client-session-changed, client-attached and client-detached, and AFTER window-pane-changed on kill-pane. Reuse the reverted fixture as a starting point (git show 0fec342 on origin/main shows what was removed). The differential runs with focus-events off AND on, with a synchronous set-buffer -a hook body recording order, and expects exactly one focus-in and one focus-out across attach / select-pane / new-window / select-window / kill-pane / detach with the option off. Record the three zz-only residues the reason names (client-focus-in on every pty attach, unfocused across switch-client, PANE_EXITED) and fix the ones that fall out of the same model.
3. clients.event-resize-context (1) and formats.window-runtime (3: format:window_bigger, format:window_offset_x, format:window_offset_y). One structural change: the daemon resolves a format client for every command expansion through current_format_client(inner, invoking_client) (daemon.rs), while the pin's format_defaults / cmd_find_best_client picks, per command, the session's most-recently-active client by activity_time, and list-windows / list-sessions rows are built with a NULL client. Make the format client per command: hook bodies (notify_insert_one_hook appends items with client NULL, resolved through cmd_find_best_client) expand #{client_tty} and #{client_width} against the activity_time winner while #{hook_client} stays the reporter; list-windows rows carry no client. Then window_bigger answers from the format client's retained viewport and null without one (the group reason already has the five-state measurement), and the offsets follow tty_window_offset1 (read tty.c: with the window fitting the viewport both are 0; when it is bigger the viewport is centred on the cursor and honours a pan; zz has no pan, so answer the centred offsets from the client viewport and the pane cursor and record precisely which pan cases stay unmatched). The event-resize differential attaches two pty clients and resizes the OLDER one, per the acceptance's second clause (the pin logged B's tty when A resized; after a key in A, resizing B logged A's). Note the copy lane touches daemon status.rs for copy-mode producers: keep your status.rs hunks to the client-resolution functions.
4. display-panes.command-template (1, a park group: remove just that slug) and display-panes.queue-semantics (1). The grammar already accepts the template; mux execution rejects the positional value. Selecting a pane substitutes its %pane id for every %%% in the template (default 'select-pane -t \\"%%%\\"'), runs it with the original queue state (cmd-display-panes.c display_panes_key: cmd_template_replace, then cmd_parse_and_insert after the display-panes item), and the issuing command sequence WAITS for the overlay to close unless -b (the wait is the same held-request mechanism display-menu uses in input_menu; -b returns at once). Prove on attached pty clients on both binaries: a template that records the chosen pane to a file, a failing template (error on the client, chain continues), the default, -b versus blocking with a chained display-message whose timing is visible (write a marker before and after), and the -d timeout.
5. display-message.pane-target-grammar (1). The whole remaining divergence is measured in the reason: from an unattached command client, -t @, {active} and {current} print 'no current client' on stderr, exit 1, and STILL run the command (S= reaches stdout); zz answers the same stdout at status 0 with no stderr. CommandResponse::Error already carries an output field: pre-resolve the three client aliases daemon-side from the invoking client (cmdq_get_client(item)->session->curw->window->active; a command client has no session, so the error fires even while another client is attached elsewhere; a componentwise -t a:1.@ is a pane-slot @ and exits 0), emit the diagnostic plus status 1 plus the expanded output, and keep a chained second command running. Attached invoker resolves its current pane. Prove both attached and unattached through the CLI on both binaries (pane-target-grammar row stays clean).
6. clients.path-encoding (1: semantic:client-environment-non-utf8), only if time remains: ClientHello's v82 UTF-8 NAME=VALUE environment needs a byte-preserving entry shape (protocol 93 -> 94, same pattern as the v92 cwd close, git show d895596 on origin/main) plus the pin's update-environment byte behaviour; measure the copy on ATTACH, not on new-session -d (the last lane's new-session probe was inconclusive and recorded nothing).
DO NOT attempt control-mode.disconnect-cancels-command-queue or pane.command-completion (split-window -W): both need a per-connection Control worker / two-phase queue foundation that has bounced three times and belongs to a design front. If your prompt -t or display-panes wait mechanism teaches you something about that foundation, write it into those two groups' reasons.
Scenario names: compat/scenarios/smoke/command-prompt-*.txt, smoke/hooks-pane-focus*.txt, smoke/client-resized-context.txt, smoke/format-window-bigger.txt, smoke/display-panes-*.txt, smoke/display-message-client-aliases.txt with fixtures; add summary rows so --check-summary stays green.`

const BATCH_COPY = COMMON + `
YOUR BATCH: the copy-mode daemon view and the copy items it unlocks, then the format enumeration, the I modifier and the trace: up to nine groups, 21 items, ordered by value. WORKDIR: zz-opus-panes (warm zz-mux/zz-protocol/zz-terminal build if present). BRANCHNAME: batch-copy-mode-view-opus.
Your zones: crates/zz-terminal, crates/zz-mux/src/{command.rs,copy_actions.rs,formats.rs,status.rs}, crates/zz-protocol/src/{catalog.rs,key.rs}. The daemon worker owns crates/zz-daemon, control_mode.rs, parser.rs and works in the prompt, hook, format-client, display-panes and target-alias regions of daemon.rs and in the client-resolution functions of daemon status.rs; the client worker owns crates/zz-client, zz-tui, zz-ui, crates/zz/src (GUI), tmux_options.rs, honest_knobs.rs, message.rs/snapshot.rs, and crates/zz-mux/src/{layout.rs,model.rs,sort.rs}. Allowed excursions for you, minimal and listed in notes: crates/zz-daemon/src/daemon.rs ONLY in the copy-session region (copy_sessions, CopySession, reconcile_copy_session, counted_copy_mode_action, the SendKeys/TerminalView/CopyModeRepeat effect arms, and one new refresh timer beside them) and crates/zz-daemon/src/status.rs ONLY for the copy-mode format producers; catalog.rs counters; message.rs only if a fact must ride the wire (say so; a 93 -> 94 bump then follows the rule). Keep every daemon hunk tight so the gate can rebase three lanes through that file.

Groups in order:
1. COPY-MODE OBSERVABILITY (prerequisite; closes three slugs): terminal.key-control (2: semantic:send-keys-no-key-count, semantic:send-keys-empty-copy-count) and copy-mode.command-fidelity (1: semantic:copy-mode-command-counts). The counts already land in the daemon (counted_copy_mode_action spends -N like wme->prefix, unit-tested against copy_cursor_y 22 -> 17 -> 16 -> 12) but nothing differential can see them: with two attached pty clients and copy-mode -t %0 at status 0, display-message -p -t %0 answers pane_in_mode=0 and empty copy_cursor_y, pane_mode and scroll_position on zz, even with -c, while the pin answers 1, copy-mode and a row. formats.rs backs pane_in_mode with a constant Zero and pane_mode with Empty; copy_cursor_x/y/line/word, scroll_position, selection_present, selection_start_x/y, selection_end_x/y, search_present and search_match are not produced at all. Produce the whole family from the daemon's copy_sessions (the single-writer map; its published revision holds the cursor, view offset and selection): a pane is in mode when any client holds a non-exiting copy session on it; the cursor and selection come from the format client's own session when it has one on that pane (the -c client, else the invoking attached client), otherwise from the earliest client's session on it. That rule is a zz decision because copy mode is per client here: write it, with the measurement, into the accepted clients.interactive-refresh group's reason (widening an accepted-native stance is legitimate; precedent 1f24a1f1) and into the two groups above. Then prove the counts differentially on both binaries with a 300-line pane and an attached pty client: copy-mode, send-keys -N 5 then -X cursor-up, -N 4 -X then cursor-up, reading #{copy_cursor_y} and #{scroll_position} through display-message -p -t %0 -c <client>. Also answer pane_mode=view-mode after run-shell -t is out of scope (clients.interactive-refresh); say so.
2. copy-mode.command-fidelity (2: flag:copy-mode:-k, flag:copy-mode:-s). Both are measured in the group reason. -k: store the bit on the daemon CopySession (MuxEffect is daemon-local, no wire) and kill the pane when the copy session actually ENDS (reconcile_copy_session seeing the terminal publish live mode again, cancel, or the client leaving the mode), through a path back into the mux that removes the pane with the pin's last-pane consequences (window, session, server); re-entering an existing mode never updates the bit (window_pane_set_mode returns early); bind-key ... copy-mode -k must parse. -s: the copy revision is built from the SOURCE pane's terminal and refuses the live re-sync whenever the source differs from the target (window_copy_refresh_start checks wme->swp); prove with two panes, the view showing the source's content while keys go to the target. Attached pty proofs on both binaries: -k followed by cancel removes the pane from list-panes, without -k it stays, -k on the last pane takes the server; -s shows the other pane's marker lines.
3. keys.copy-mode-unsupported-default-actions (2: key:copy-mode-vi:r, key:copy-mode:r) via refresh-toggle / refresh-on / refresh-off: a daemon-owned re-sync of the frozen revision on a 50ms timer (window_copy_do_refresh: re-clone the backing, keep the view offset, follow the bottom only when oy is 0 and the cursor is on the last row; refuse to start for a -s view and for view-mode; skip a tick while a selection or a cursor drag is live). Then install both r keys with the pin's stored 'send-keys -X refresh-toggle' shape and nonrepeat metadata (the compat_manifest_tests key partition counts move). Prove with a pane printing a line every 200ms: #{scroll_position} holds at 0 with refresh off, climbs after refresh-on, freezes after refresh-toggle (the reason's measurement), on both binaries through the new formats from group 1.
4. copy-mode.action-fidelity (1: semantic:copy-mode-copy-format-and-destination) and terminal.key-control (1: semantic:send-keys-copy-command-shape). Map the four unmapped copy names (copy-line, copy-line-and-cancel, copy-pipe-line, copy-pipe-line-and-cancel: window_copy_cmd_copy_line copies the whole logical line the cursor is on without a selection, -and-cancel exits, -pipe runs the command with the copied text on stdin) in copy_actions.rs, the terminal engine and the daemon effect arm; the copy-command shape item is scoped by commands.tmux-name-extensions to those four plus the pin's redraw after a parse failure (window_copy_command: an unknown -X action is reported and the mode redrawn, not left). Prove the copied buffer contents (list-buffers / show-buffer) and the pipe output file on both binaries with an attached client.
5. keys.copy-mode-binding-fidelity (2: binding:copy-mode-vi:#, binding:copy-mode-vi:*). The other fourteen stay on the accepted command-prompt -P decision; do not touch them. These two need send-keys -F (catalog.rs currently records -F as a 'compatibility no-op'; the pin's cmd_send_keys_exec expands every key argument as a format in the target pane's context when -F is given, before key lookup) and the #{copy_cursor_word} format from group 1, so the pin's stored binding shape (send-keys -FX search-backward '#{copy_cursor_word}' and search-forward for *) can be installed verbatim; zz's native search-backward-cursor-word actions stay for the native tables. The compat_manifest_tests tracked == divergent key counts move with the close; prove the rendered command through list-keys on both binaries and the behaviour through an attached copy-mode search.
6. display-message.format-listing (2: flag:display-message:-a, semantic:display-message-format-listing). The measurement is in the reason: format_each walks the 198-entry table alphabetically skipping NULL callbacks, then the tree pass (command=display-message last); a fresh detached server prints 143 lines for -t %0. zz's expander models no null, so first give FORMAT_VARIABLES a per-variable, per-context 'declines' answer (a producer that returns None rather than empty: the pin's format_cb_* return NULL when the context lacks the object or the fact, e.g. client_* without a client, copy_cursor_* outside a mode, window_bigger without a client) and then the ordered walk. Measure the pin's exact listing on a throwaway server in a session, a window and a pane context, with and without an attached client, and drive the listings on both binaries through the differential; names the pin prints and zz does not (or the reverse) are recorded per name in the reason with their owning group, and the acceptance clause about the set difference is re-scoped to that per-name record with the trail. Close -a when the order rule, the line shape and every name both sides define match; close the semantic slug only if the listing is identical in every probed context.
7. formats.modifier-fidelity (1: semantic:format-modifier-client-interrogation, the I modifier). The grammar is fully measured in the reason (I/f feature, I/c terminfo capability, I/e environment; last-wins flag order c, f, e; literal body; empty with no client). f and e are backed today by the daemon's client_termfeatures and environment rows. For c, tty_term_has_name needs a terminfo source: the term crate 0.7 is already in Cargo.lock (term::terminfo::TermInfo::from_name parses the compiled terminfo entry for a TERM name, honouring TERMINFO and TERMINFO_DIRS); load it lazily per client TERM in the daemon (or use the terminfo crate if term's parser is insufficient; either is allowed as a direct zz-daemon dependency, say which in notes) and answer capability presence by the pin's rule (read tty-term.c tty_term_has_name and the tty_term_codes table: the name is the tmux code name, not the terminfo name, so map through the table). Prove I/f:RGB, I/f:256, I/f:nosuchfeature, I/c:smcup, I/c:colors, I/c:nosuchcap, I/e:FOO, an unset name, the no-client case, the literal body, and the flag-order cases on both binaries with an attached pty client whose TERM is a name both boxes have (xterm-256color).
8. Last, only if time remains: copy-mode.action-fidelity (1: semantic:copy-mode-logical-line-and-mode-keys; the mode-keys value has to travel to the copy session and the engine must answer the eleven window-copy.c branches the reason lists; the onemore rule is six of them), terminal.key-control (1: flag:send-keys:-K, a re-entrant replay of the selected client's key-table handler after the state lock drops; -K with no positional key replays the invoking queue key), display-message.verbose-trace (2: a structured trace sink in the expander printing the pin's format_log lines for -v; measure the exact text on the pin first). Record precisely what is missing for each one you do not reach.
Scenario names: compat/scenarios/smoke/copy-mode-*.txt, smoke/send-keys-*.txt, smoke/copy-mode-refresh.txt, smoke/format-listing*.txt, smoke/format-modifier-interrogate.txt with fixtures; add summary rows so --check-summary stays green. catalog.rs counters and compat_manifest_tests.rs counts move with every promotion.`

const BATCH_CLIENT = COMMON + `
YOUR BATCH: the two client-consumed options, the pin's pane border chrome, the menu pointer and paste rules, the chooser key vocabulary, and the remaining popup behaviour: up to five groups, 16 items, ordered by value. WORKDIR: zz-opus-termopts (warm zz-tui/zz/zz-terminal build; it exists here and is clean). BRANCHNAME: batch-client-chrome-overlays-opus.
Your zones: crates/zz-client, crates/zz-tui, crates/zz-ui, crates/zz/src (GUI; NOT control_mode.rs), crates/zz-mux/src/{tmux_options.rs,honest_knobs.rs,layout.rs,model.rs,sort.rs}, crates/zz-protocol/src/{message.rs,snapshot.rs,lib.rs}. The daemon worker owns crates/zz-daemon (prompt, hook, format-client, display-panes, target-alias regions), control_mode.rs, parser.rs; the copy worker owns crates/zz-terminal, command.rs, copy_actions.rs, formats.rs, mux status.rs, catalog.rs, key.rs, and the copy-session region of daemon.rs. Allowed excursions for you, minimal and listed in notes: crates/zz-daemon/src/daemon.rs ONLY in the chooser session, menu, popup, pane-appearance, snapshot-publishing and option-snapshot regions; catalog.rs promotions; command.rs parse hunks and TMUX_OPTION_CONSUMERS registrations; crates/zz/src/lib.rs for the CLI launcher (default-client-command). Keep every daemon hunk tight so the gate can rebase three lanes through that file.

Groups in order:
1. options.client-attach-and-focus (2, a park group: option:default-client-command, option:focus-follows-mouse). The recipe is in the reason. default-client-command: server_client_default_command fetches the option (default new-session) as a command list whenever a client arrives with no command, and substitutes the read-only refusal when the client is read-only and the list is not all CMD_READONLY commands; zz's CLI hard-codes new-session. Have the launcher ask the daemon for the option before it synthesizes new-session and run the parsed list through the same path an explicit argument takes; prove with a bare client on both binaries under set -g default-client-command 'display-message -p custom', a multi-command list, and a read-only client (-r) against a non-read-only list (pin: refusal text and status). focus-follows-mouse: publish the value on the option snapshot the clients already receive, gate the GPUI pane hover handler and the TUI pointer route on it (the pin turns a MOUSEMOVE over an inactive pane into window_set_active_pane and forces MODE_MOUSE_ALL on the client); prove on the raw TUI with SGR motion bytes on the pty on both binaries through #{pane_active} / list-panes, on and off.
2. options.pane-border-chrome (6, a park group: presentation:border-style-owner-z-order, option:pane-border-lines, option:pane-border-indicators, option:pane-border-status, option:pane-border-format, presentation:renderer-style-residue), in this order. (a) z-order: the pin's same-side border ties follow the window's mutable pane list order (TAILQ in window.c: swap-pane, join-pane and a serialized select-layout reorder it; read window_pane_insert / layout_set and measure with a three-pane join and swap); add a per-window tiled z-order to MuxSnapshot (protocol 93 -> 94, appended), maintain it in mux-model at exactly those commands, and mark the owner in the raw TUI's per-span style ownership; the 2026-08-31 per-span differential (border spans) plus an attached-TUI ANSI colour assertion for the A|(B/C) ties on both binaries is the proof. (b) pane-border-lines: the resolved window option needs a route to the client: a snapshot field (same bump), a lines table single/double/heavy/simple/number/spaces/none beside the popup's, and junction glyphs chosen per cell the way screen_redraw_type_of_cell does (read screen-redraw.c; zz's divider list currently overwrites one divider with the other at a crossing, so teach paint_workspace about crossings); prove each value on an attached TUI client with a T-split and a cross-split, capturing the outer pane in the nested rig. (c) pane-border-indicators colour / arrows / both / off: active-pane marking. (d) pane-border-status top / bottom + pane-border-format: the pin's row is real geometry (measured: an 80x24 horizontal split's panes go from 40x24 at 0,0 and 39x24 at 41,0 to 40x23 at 0,1 and 39x23 at 41,1 under top, 40x23 at 0,0 under bottom), so the mux layout reserves the row (layout.rs; read layout_fix_panes and window.c's pane-border-status handling) and #{pane_height} / #{pane_top} prove it differentially without a client; the daemon expands pane-border-format per pane (default '#{?pane_active,#[reverse],}#{pane_index} \\"#{pane_title}\\"' plus mouse ranges when the mouse is on) and publishes the text on the pane snapshot for the raw TUI to draw on that row; GPUI stays theme-owned by product decision. (e) renderer-style-residue: the acceptance clause asks that the residue NAME what is left; write the exact list (attributes and dim, #() shell branches in style strings, non-colour border attributes and fills, per-window mode styles, copy-mode position style and format, TUI reverse-video flattening, the pane-colours default/terminal slot) into the reason and close the slug only if the clause is satisfied by that record; otherwise leave it with the list.
3. display-menu.behavior-fidelity (2: semantic:display-menu-mouse-policy, semantic:display-menu-paste-close-ordering). Both are read off the pin in the reason (menu_key_cb: a no-button move is highlight-only, MENU_NOMOUSE rules, the hit box px..px+4+width by py+1..py+1+count-1 excluding the title row and bottom border, outside-release closes a non-stay-open menu, inside-release chooses the row; a KEYC_PASTE_START is matched against nothing and the menu stays open with the paste swallowed whole and nothing reaching the pane). Drive an attached TUI client on both binaries with SGR mouse bytes and a bracketed paste while a menu is up, observing through the row's command writing a file and the pane's content (capture-pane); implement the same policy in the daemon menu region and the TUI input route.
4. choosers.command-flags (2: flag:choose-tree:-y, semantic:chooser-key-vocabulary). The pin's mode_tree_key answers t, T, C-t, K, J, S-Up, S-Down, O, r, F1, C-h, M-- and M-+, and window_tree_key adds m, x, X and ':'; x and X raise the 'Kill window?' / 'Kill session?' confirm prompt through the status prompt and -y auto-accepts it (PROMPT_ACCEPT). Read mode-tree.c and window-tree.c, implement the vocabulary in the daemon chooser session with both clients rendering it, and prove each key on attached pty clients on both binaries through the tree's effect (tagging, sort order O, kill with and without -y, ':' command prompt on the selected item). Record the two chooser residues the reason already names (display-menu -c over an open chooser; a -k chooser replaced by another overlay) and fix them if the vocabulary work makes it cheap.
5. display-popup.behavior-fidelity (4: semantic:display-popup-kitty-images, semantic:display-popup-context-menu, semantic:display-popup-border-drag, semantic:display-popup-to-pane). kitty-images first: it is a zz-native contract (the pinned tree has no kitty or sixel support at all), so prove zz's own rendering, replacement and close cleanup of a kitty image inside a popup on an attached raw TUI client (crates/zz-tui/src/kitty.rs) with a test that asserts the placements and their removal. Then the pointer items, which need a menu OVER a popup: popup_key_cb's mouse arm raises popup_menu_items (Close / Paste / Fill Space / Centre / To Horizontal Pane / To Vertical Pane) on button 3 on the left or top border or outside the popup; a meta drag or a border drag moves with button 1 and resizes with button 3; popup_make_pane hands the popup's job and screen to a freshly split pane through job_transfer. any_overlay_present refuses a second overlay per client today: build the nested case for a menu over a popup (menu closes back to the popup), then context-menu, border-drag and to-pane, each proved with SGR mouse bytes on attached clients on both binaries and observed through list-panes and capture-pane. Record precise pin measurements for the ones you cannot close.
Scenario names: compat/scenarios/smoke/default-client-command.txt, smoke/focus-follows-mouse.txt, smoke/pane-border-*.txt, smoke/display-menu-mouse.txt, smoke/display-menu-paste.txt, smoke/chooser-keys.txt, smoke/display-popup-*.txt with fixtures; add summary rows so --check-summary stays green.`

const REVIEW_COMMON = `You are an adversarial code reviewer for the zz tmux-compat campaign (repo demfabris/zz). A worker just pushed a campaign branch; your verdict decides what the integration gate trusts. Read-only toward history: NEVER push, NEVER commit, NEVER touch the board or GitHub issues, never edit ${M.root} itself.

SETUP: git -C ${M.root} fetch origin +refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main '+refs/heads/campaign/*:refs/remotes/origin/campaign/*' (SSH origin works; HTTPS https://github.com/demfabris/zz.git is the fallback if it hangs). Scratch worktree at the branch tip: git -C ${M.root} worktree add ${M.dev}/REVIEWDIR <branch-tip-sha> (if REVIEWDIR exists from a previous cycle and is clean, checkout --detach the tip there instead to reuse its build); remove a worktree you created when done (worktree remove --force). This box: /bin/bash is 3.2, use ${M.bash} or python3 for helpers; APFS refuses non-UTF-8 names.

MACHINE ETIQUETTE: others may still be working on ${M.cores} cores. cargo test -p <pkg> --jobs ${M.workerJobs} -- --test-threads=${M.workerThreads} only, no workspace-scale anything, cargo output to a log file + check exit code. Timeout-guard cli_binary tests (wait_exit can hang under load). Throwaway pin servers -L zzprobe-$$ only, kill after; never kill servers you did not start (${M.protected}), never pkill tmux or zz.

METHOD, in order of value:
1. CONTRACT AUDIT: for every closed OR relocated slug (worker report + git diff origin/main...HEAD -- compat/tmux-gaps.json), find the proof in the diff that asserts the group acceptance clause. A test asserting zz behavior with no pin derivation is a defect; quote the clause. A relocation into an accepted-native group (or a widened accepted stance) is legitimate only when that group's reason states the measured pin behavior and the product stance, and the manifest counts still enforce it. A RE-SCOPED acceptance clause is legitimate only when the reason records the old clause, the refuting measurement, and the probe; re-run that probe on the pin yourself. Anything proved for copy mode, prompts, choosers, menus, popups, focus, or mouse must drive a real attached client; a headless or detached proof of those is a defect.
2. PROOFS AT TIP: run the worker's claimed proof suites yourself at the branch tip (cargo test -p for every touched crate; the named scenarios). ALSO run cargo test -p zz-daemon --lib whenever the diff touches crates/zz-mux/src/command.rs, model.rs or layout.rs, even if the worker did not claim it. Any red at tip is a blocker regardless of the report.
3. ORACLE SPOT-CHECKS: 3-5 riskiest claims (option gating, queue and hook order, precedence, counts, prompt keys, mouse hit boxes, layout geometry, transitions) verified against the pinned binary yourself (${M.root}/compat/.cache/tmux-src/tmux; the pinned C source sits beside the binary, read it when subtle). Past best catches were pin-side gating the worker's fixture had configured away (focus-events on), a mode reset relying on a control sequence the pinned libghostty ignores, a hook mechanism the pin does not have (the notified client as format client), and a fixture step that landed in the wrong snapshot on BOTH binaries: look for the configuration that would make a fixture blind and for a probe that passes for the wrong reason.
4. TEST HONESTY: run the branch's new/changed tests and its scenarios (${RUN_ENV} compat/run.sh --strict-geometry <names>). Failing/ignored/tautological = blocker. Check the durable registry resolution carries every divergence the worker disclosed in its notes.
5. INVARIANTS: zone discipline (out-of-zone files listed in notes); wire rule (wire-reachable change => complete 93->94 bump incl. hex 0x5D->0x5E fixture and knowledge mirrors, changelog says inserted vs appended honestly); no code comments; doc comments still attached to the fn they describe; no attribution trailers; registry round-trips (python3 -m json.tool); tracker check green on the branch; --check-summary green if scenarios were added.
6. CALIBRATION: default to refuting each close, but confirmed_defects only with PROOF (probe, failing rerun, quoted contradiction). Suspicion goes in notes. blocker = wrong close or would break main; must-fix = gate applies before merge; nit = mention. When a blocker is a wrong close, say whether reverting that commit applies cleanly at tip and what it takes with it (that is the fix the gate will use).
VERDICT: approve / approve-with-fixes / reject (a blocker the gate cannot fix in minutes). checks_run lists exact commands. Thorough but bounded: well under an hour.`

const LANES = [
  { key: 'daemon', prompt: BATCH_DAEMON, lock: 'F-DAEMON-PROMPT-FOCUS', reviewdir: 'zz-review-dint' },
  { key: 'copy', prompt: BATCH_COPY, lock: 'F-COPY-MODE-DAEMON-VIEW', reviewdir: 'zz-review-copy' },
  { key: 'client', prompt: BATCH_CLIENT, lock: 'F-CLIENT-CHROME-OVERLAYS', reviewdir: 'zz-review-client' },
]

log('Cycle 7: daemon prompt/hooks/format client/display-panes (15 items) + copy-mode daemon view/formats (21) + client chrome/overlays (16); Fable review per lane, Fable gate')
const laneResults = await pipeline(
  LANES,
  lane => agent(lane.prompt, { label: `worker:${lane.key}`, phase: 'Work', model: 'opus', schema: WORKER_SCHEMA })
    .then(w => ({ lane, worker: w })),
  r => {
    if (!r || !r.worker || !r.worker.branch) return r
    const reviewPrompt = REVIEW_COMMON + `

LANE: ${r.lane.key}. BRANCH: ${r.worker.branch}. REVIEWDIR: ${r.lane.reviewdir}.
WORKER REPORT (verify, do not trust):
${JSON.stringify(r.worker, null, 2)}`
    return agent(reviewPrompt, { label: `review:${r.lane.key}`, phase: 'Review', model: 'fable', schema: REVIEW_SCHEMA })
      .then(review => ({ ...r, review }))
  }
)
const summaries = laneResults.filter(Boolean).filter(r => r.worker && r.worker.branch)
  .map(r => ({ key: r.lane.key, lock_front: r.lane.lock, review: r.review || null, ...r.worker }))
log(`Lanes complete: ${summaries.length}/3 branches pushed and reviewed. Integrating serially.`)

phase('Integrate')
const gatePrompt = `You are the integration gate for the zz tmux-compat campaign (repo demfabris/zz, board = GitHub issue 7). All workers and reviewers are done; you run ALONE on this ${M.machine}, full speed. Integrate IN THIS ORDER: daemon first (the largest daemon.rs footprint), copy second (its daemon excursions rebase onto it), client third (it carries the protocol bump and the snapshot fields). One gate per branch, and push main as soon as each lane's gate is green (if you die mid-run the orchestrator resumes from what already landed).

Lane summaries, worker report + Fable review verdict each:
${JSON.stringify(summaries, null, 2)}

REVIEW VERDICTS BIND YOU: approve-with-fixes => apply every must-fix on the branch (own follow-up commit) before its gate, re-running the reviewer's failing probe to prove each fix. reject => do NOT merge; post the blockers as a board note on the lock front, leave the branch, continue. A blocker you can genuinely fix in minutes may be fixed and merged with the probe re-run as proof; a wrong close whose revert applies cleanly is such a fix (precedent 0fec342 + 9cab1fa: revert, then a records commit that puts the reviewer's measurement into the group reason and acceptance). review_actions must account for every confirmed defect. Missing review (null) => do a compressed contract audit yourself before gating.

BOARD IDENTITY: ZZ_BOARD_HOLDER=${M.holder} python3 compat/board.py <cmd> from inside a repo checkout. Verbs: release/withdraw REQUIRE --reason; note takes --note; candidate takes --commit --branch --base + repeatable --proof; integrated takes --merge + optional --gate; renew <FRONT> --lease 2h (renew sets expiry to now + lease; always give a unit); front needs --contract --zones [--priority --kind --deps --notes]; withdraw needs TRIAGE held. The orchestrator holds the three lock fronts F-DAEMON-PROMPT-FOCUS, F-COPY-MODE-DAEMON-VIEW, F-CLIENT-CHROME-OVERLAYS (6h leases from launch, the orchestrator renews them; expired => claim back as ${M.holder} before that lane's ledger step).

NETWORK GIT: origin is SSH and works non-interactively. Fetch git fetch origin +refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main '+refs/heads/campaign/*:refs/remotes/origin/campaign/*'; push git push origin HEAD:main. If a network command hangs ~30s, kill it and retry once over HTTPS (https://github.com/demfabris/zz.git). The shared checkout's local main branch may be stale; never use it, never touch it.
knowledge/tmux/gaps.md is generated: regenerate with tmux-tracker.py write-report on every conflict, never hand-merge it.
PROTOCOL RECONCILE: if MULTIPLE branches bumped PROTOCOL_VERSION to 94, the rebase keeps ONE constant at 94 with every changelog entry preserved as separate v94 bullets and the hunt_claims fixture updated once (0x5E); verify cargo test -p zz-protocol after reconciling. catalog.rs counters and compat_manifest_tests.rs counts conflict between lanes routinely: resolve by recounting, then cargo test -p zz-mux and -p zz-protocol, and grep the tree for conflict markers before building (a cycle-6 rebase once committed markers into catalog.rs). Three lanes touched crates/zz-daemon/src/daemon.rs in different regions; rebase conflicts there are resolved by keeping both hunks and letting the workspace run judge.

THIS BOX: /bin/bash is 3.2 and lacks mapfile; write the shard runner for ${M.bash} or python3 (a cycle-6 shard script under /bin/bash ran the full corpus eight times concurrently). APFS refuses non-UTF-8 names: smoke/client-non-utf8-cwd is environmental here unless the daemon lane's fixture guard landed (then it must be clean). Never touch ${M.protected}.

PER BRANCH, in order:
1. Fresh worktree: git -C ${M.root} worktree add ${M.root}-gate-<key> origin/main (remove leftovers with --force first). Rebase branch onto origin/main; compat/tmux-gaps.json conflicts between lanes are normal: merge both sides.
2. First branch only: claim MAIN --lease 2h; hold across all, renew before each subsequent gate, release after the ledger recompute.
3. Code-branch gate stages, in order:
   a. cargo test --workspace --all-features --no-fail-fast --jobs ${M.gateJobs} -- --test-threads=${M.gateThreads} > log 2>&1 (check exit code; never pipe through tail). Timeout-guard: wait_exit_holds_the_control_process_until_a_second_blank_line can HANG under load; if the run wedges >20min with no output, sample the process; a lost-wakeup hang there counts as the known flake (verify solo).
   b. cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings
   c. ${RUN_ENV} compat/run.sh --strict-geometry --delta origin/main..HEAD --commands <lane touched_commands>. Run --list TWICE and reconcile against git diff --name-only for compat/scenarios (one --list once came back stale and missed six new scenarios); shard up to ${M.shards} concurrent run.sh invocations over DISJOINT scenario subsets with separate result logs; run smoke/source-replay-diagnostics SOLO after the shards (pin-side crash under load); any divergence re-runs alone before being called real.
   d. python3 compat/tmux-tracker.py check && python3 compat/board_test.py && compat/run.sh --check-summary
4. Flake rules: lone timing test failing loaded + passing exact-solo = flake, proceed (known list: copy-mode reconcile, attached_client_extents…, client_focus_closes…, history_request_is_guarded…, daemon_native_split_resize_commits_exactly…, nested_alias_queue_bubbles_shutdown…, control_sourced_run_shell_closes_before_raw_output…, request_full_enqueues_only_the_requested_visible_pane, display_menu_resize_lifecycle::a_resize_moves_the_menu…, zz-terminal pty_output_drains_while_the_input_writer_is_backpressured, wait_exit… hang, concurrent_default_interactive… "not a terminal" incl. misattribution). Anything else red = real: fix if minutes, else SKIP branch (no push), record, continue.
5. Push main. Non-fast-forward: fetch; user-authored commits + conflict-free disjoint rebase => bounded rerun (lane package tests + its scenarios), push. Never force. Campaign branches rewritten by the rebase stay at their old tips on origin (never force them); say so in the report.
6. Ledger per successful push against that lane's lock_front: candidate (--commit tip --branch campaign/<name> --base <pre-push main> --proof per stage), note (--note: groups covered, slugs closed/relocated, reviewer verdict + what you did about defects), integrated (--merge <sha> --gate "workspace+clippy+delta green"), release (--reason "batch integrated at <sha>").
7. After ALL branches, holding MAIN: recompute TMUX_COMPAT_TRACKER.md from the merged registry: the four headline lines (Campaign delivery, Live work "<open> OPEN + <blocked> BLOCKED = <sum>", Ledger settlement "100 x (<closed> CLOSED + <accepted> ACCEPTED) / (<closed> CLOSED + <len(gaps)> LIVE)" one decimal, Exit evidence scenarios/steps from --check-summary), the Current checkpoint rows, the Campaign dashboard table rows (Live unresolved, Latest differential, Differential SHA-256 = sha256 of compat/results/summary.md, Ledger settlement), the Ledger settlement calculation block, and a new "### <date> cycle-7 integration checkpoint" table like the cycle-6 one above it. Records commit ("Recompute the live ledger after the cycle-7 merges"), push, release MAIN.
8. Claim TRIAGE. Withdraw fronts fully mooted by the merges, slug-verified one by one against the merged registry: candidates F-DISPLAY-MESSAGE-PANE-TARGETS-V3 (daemon lane group 5), F-KEY-CONTROL-V3 (copy lane groups 1, 4 and 8), F-PANE-BORDER-LINES-TILED then F-PANE-BORDER-ZORDER (client lane group 2; LINES-TILED depends on ZORDER, so withdraw it first). F-PANE-COMMAND-COMPLETION and the F-SPLIT-MUX-* chain stay. If a worker's skip reasons prove a group contract is unprovable as written, post that as a residual on the relevant front (or front 'none'). Release TRIAGE.
9. python3 compat/progress.py, full output.
10. Remove only your own zz-gate-* worktrees. Leave zz-opus-* and zz-review-*.

Never stash/reset anything in ${M.root}. Never kill tmux or zz servers you did not start (${M.protected}). Report via structured output: per branch merged/sha/gate_summary/review_actions/flakes, full progress output, board records, problems.`

const gate = summaries.length
  ? await agent(gatePrompt, { label: 'gate:serial', phase: 'Integrate', model: 'fable', schema: GATE_SCHEMA })
  : null
if (!gate) log('No branches to integrate: all workers came back empty.')

return { lanes: laneResults, gate }
